## Users

Self sign-up is off. Invite users from the Cognito console or the CLI. Mark the email as verified,
otherwise password reset does not work:

```bash
aws cognito-idp admin-create-user --user-pool-id <UserPoolId> --username <email> --user-attributes Name=email,Value=<email> Name=email_verified,Value=true --desired-delivery-mediums EMAIL
```

Put administrators in the `admin` group; the group appears in the access token's `cognito:groups` claim.

## MFA

{%- if poolSettings.mfa == "required" %}

MFA is required. At first sign-in, Managed Login shows a QR code for an authenticator app (Google
Authenticator, 1Password, Authy, ...) and asks for a code on every sign-in after that. If a user
loses their device, clear their authenticator so Managed Login asks them to set it up again:

```bash
aws cognito-idp admin-set-user-mfa-preference --user-pool-id <UserPoolId> --username <email> --software-token-mfa-settings Enabled=false
```
{%- elsif poolSettings.mfa == "optional" %}

MFA is optional with an authenticator app. Managed Login does not offer MFA setup unless MFA is
required, so users can only turn it on through a setup screen in your app (`AssociateSoftwareToken`
and `VerifySoftwareToken`, which need the `aws.cognito.signin.user.admin` scope on the client). To
make everyone use it, set `mfa: Mfa.REQUIRED` in `src/cognito-stack.ts`.
{%- else %}

MFA is off. To turn it on, set `mfa: Mfa.REQUIRED` and `mfaSecondFactor: { sms: false, otp: true }`
in `src/cognito-stack.ts`. Managed Login then walks every user through authenticator app setup at
their next sign-in.
{%- endif %}

## Deleting

The user pool is kept when the stack is deleted, so users are not lost by accident. Delete it by
hand in the console if it really should go. Deletion protection is on, so turn it off in the pool's
settings first.
